For each item, the marginal benefit of the t-th copy (t=1..d_i) is 2 copies (1 purchased + 1 free), cost p_i
For the (d_i+1)-th and beyond copies, marginal benefit is 1 copy (purchased only), cost p_i

So we have n * d_i potential high-value "slots" (each giving 2 copies), and unlimited low-value slots (each giving 1 copy)

We want to select slots in order of best marginal benefit per cost:

- High-value slots: benefit 2/p_i per p_i cost
- Low-value slots: benefit 1/p_i per p_i cost

Since 2/p_i > 1/p_i, all high-value slots are better than all low-value slots.

So we should take as many high-value slots as possible, in order of best 2/p_i (i.e., smallest p_i), then take low-value slots with best 1/p_i (smallest p_i).

